From 71bfcf2c06fb53a94611518117553772450c88bb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: osy <osy@turing.llc>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 11:17:16 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] edid: carry the host refresh rate consistently, up to
 DisplayID 2.0

Two places in the generated EDID contradicted the preferred detailed
timing, which already carried the host's refresh rate.

The standard timing descriptors hardcoded 60 Hz in their six-bit rate
field, so a guest that builds its mode list from them gave every mode
60 Hz. Windows does exactly that, and drove a 120 Hz display at 60.
Encode the rate the UI reported, saturated to the 60..123 Hz the field
spans, and keep the 60 Hz DMT modes when the UI reported nothing rather
than the 75 Hz placeholder the detailed timing is synthesised from.

The display range limits descriptor declared a fixed 50..125 Hz and
30..160 kHz. A host display faster than 125 Hz, or a large mode whose
line rate passes 160 kHz, puts the preferred timing outside the range
the same EDID declares. Windows rejects an EDID that contradicts itself:
it drops the monitor's mode list for a default one, the adapter stops
driving the display, and D3D device creation fails with E_FAIL. Widen
the range to contain the preferred timing as the guest derives it from
the detailed timing, rounding the minimum down and the maximum up, and
use EDID 1.4's +255 offset flags for a maximum that does not fit a
byte; 510 is the most the descriptor can express.

A rate in the standard timings that no DMT mode carries asks the guest
to derive a raster from a formula, so declare one, together with the
continuous frequency feature bit that has to accompany it. Declare CVT
rather than the GTF that EDID 1.4 deprecates, and give the preferred
timing the horizontal blanking of CVT reduced blanking v2. The narrow
blanking keeps the pixel clock of common fast modes inside the
655.35 MHz a detailed timing descriptor holds, so 2560x1440 at 144 Hz
or 1920x1080 at 240 Hz stay in the base block, and under the 2550 MHz
the range limits can express.

Past the range limits descriptor, 510 Hz, 510 kHz per line and
2550 MHz, the base block cannot describe the timing at all, and neither
can a DisplayID 1.3 section, whose type I timing counts the pixel clock
in tens of kHz and which carries no range of its own. A 4K display past
about 220 Hz, whose line rate passes 510 kHz, has no way to state what
it does. Emit a DisplayID 2.0 section for such a timing: its type VII
timing counts the pixel clock in kHz, and the dynamic video timing
range limits block beside it holds a 24 bit pixel clock and a ten bit
vertical rate, and constrains no line rate at all. Only such a timing
gets one. A guest has understood type I timings far longer than type
VII ones, which Linux learned to read in 5.18, so anything a 1.3
section can describe keeps the 1.3 section it always had.

The type I and type VII timings share one writer, which stores the
clock one less than it counts as DisplayID requires; the 1.3 section
used to publish it 10 kHz high.

None of this applies to a host that reports no rate, or one whose rate
the standard timings already carry. The EDID those produce stays byte
for byte the one QEMU has always generated, unless its range limits
already contradicted its preferred timing, as a 4K display's 167 kHz
line rate did at the 75 Hz placeholder, or it carries that DisplayID
clock.

Have vfio-display pass on the rate the UI reports as well, and give
qemu-edid a -r option to generate an EDID for a given rate.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5-1
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
---
