From ae92cdc41fb0b0dab36c6ac724e1729ffc47ef18 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: osy <osy@turing.llc>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 11:52:08 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] usb-storage: don't park a CSW that arrives while the command
 completes

usb_msd_command_complete() completed the pending data packet before it
dropped s->req.  Completing a packet lets the host controller process
the next TRB on that endpoint synchronously, and when the guest has
already queued its CSW request, usb_msd_handle_data() runs re-entrantly
in CSW mode, sees s->req still set and parks the packet as "still in
flight".  The command has finished by then, so nothing ever completes
that packet: the guest times out the transfer, stops the endpoint and
resets the device before it retries.

Windows' USB storage timeout is 10 s, so a Windows install from a USB
CD stalled ~10 s after every short burst of reads (ep_stop, msd reset,
slot reset each time).  The window is narrow, but with the HVF in-kernel
GICv3 the guest takes the interrupt and queues the CSW fast enough to
hit it on nearly every burst: copying Windows files went from ~160 s to
~1230 s.

Release the request before completing the packet, so a packet handled
re-entrantly sees the same state as one that arrives after
usb_msd_command_complete() returns: the CSW gets its status immediately.
The SCSI layer holds its own reference across the complete callback,
and nothing after the release touches the request, so dropping ours
earlier is safe.  The same install now copies its files in ~135 s with
the in-kernel GIC, with no timeout resets.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
---
 hw/usb/dev-storage.c | 12 ++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/hw/usb/dev-storage.c b/hw/usb/dev-storage.c
index 4f1e8b7f6c..aac4b26424 100644
--- a/hw/usb/dev-storage.c
+++ b/hw/usb/dev-storage.c
@@ -273,6 +273,16 @@ void usb_msd_command_complete(SCSIRequest *req, size_t resid)
     s->csw.residue = cpu_to_le32(s->data_len);
     s->csw.status = req->status != 0;
 
+    /*
+     * Release the request before completing the packet: completing it can
+     * make the host controller process the guest's already queued CSW
+     * request synchronously, and usb_msd_handle_data() would park that
+     * packet as "still in flight" while s->req is set, with nothing left
+     * to complete it.
+     */
+    scsi_req_unref(req);
+    s->req = NULL;
+
     if (s->packet) {
         if (s->data_len == 0 && s->mode == USB_MSDM_DATAOUT) {
             /* A deferred packet with no write data remaining must be
@@ -300,8 +310,6 @@ void usb_msd_command_complete(SCSIRequest *req, size_t resid)
     } else if (s->data_len == 0) {
         s->mode = USB_MSDM_CSW;
     }
-    scsi_req_unref(req);
-    s->req = NULL;
 }
 
 void usb_msd_request_cancelled(SCSIRequest *req)
